WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.280
A fetus does not use its lungs to exchange gas, so where does its blood go?

2
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.400
Researchers injected radioactive microspheres into fetal lambs and counted them

3
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:19.280
organ by organ. Roughly 40 to 45 percent of combined ventricular output went to the

4
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.200
placenta. Under 10 percent went to the lungs.

5
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.140
Those are sheep numbers from an instrumented preparation, and human proportions are

6
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.140
inferred from imaging.

7
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:33.633
Three shunts route fetal blood past the liver and the lungs; the fetal lungs are

8
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.413
fluid filled, with high resistance.

9
00:00:36.093 --> 00:00:40.653
The ductus venosus carries a variable share of the umbilical vein blood past the

10
00:00:40.653 --> 00:00:42.993
liver, toward the inferior vena cava.

11
00:00:43.633 --> 00:00:49.133
The foramen ovale, an opening in the atrial septum, passes a stream from the right

12
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:50.493
atrium to the left.

13
00:00:51.073 --> 00:00:55.873
Most of the blood leaving the pulmonary artery crosses the ductus arteriosus into

14
00:00:55.873 --> 00:01:01.893
the aorta. Umbilical arteries carry blood back to the placenta, a low resistance bed

15
00:01:01.893 --> 00:01:03.453
in parallel with the fetal body.

16
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:08.567
At birth, air fills the lungs and pulmonary vascular resistance falls.

17
00:01:09.147 --> 00:01:14.307
Pulmonary blood flow rises, and pulmonary venous return raises pressure in the left

18
00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:20.727
atrium. Once left atrial pressure exceeds right atrial pressure, the flap of septum

19
00:01:20.727 --> 00:01:22.787
primum is pressed against the septum.

20
00:01:23.147 --> 00:01:26.407
The foramen ovale then stops carrying a right to left stream.

21
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:31.230
Losing the placenta removes a low resistance circuit, so systemic vascular

22
00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.790
resistance and ventricular afterload rise.

23
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:39.650
Oxygen constricts the ductus arteriosus, shown directly on fresh human ductal rings

24
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.050
studied outside the body.

25
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.690
These endpoints do not arrive together.

26
00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:49.570
In 50 healthy term neonates followed by ultrasound, the ductus arteriosus had closed

27
00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.810
in 94 % before day three.

28
00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:58.290
Only 12 % had a closed ductus venosus by then, and Doppler found foramen ovale flow

29
00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.290
in every infant.

30
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:04.813
Instrumented preterm lambs, six in each group, were clamped before ventilation or

31
00:02:04.813 --> 00:02:06.433
ventilated before clamping.

32
00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:11.573
Clamping first dropped mean right ventricular output by about 66 percent.

33
00:02:12.253 --> 00:02:18.093
The group means fell from 114.6 to 38.8 millilitres per minute per kilogram.

34
00:02:18.873 --> 00:02:20.833
Heart rate fell about 40 percent too.

35
00:02:21.613 --> 00:02:24.193
Ventilating first gave stable flows and pressures.

36
00:02:25.083 --> 00:02:30.123
The reading is that, before aeration, umbilical venous return supplies much of the

37
00:02:30.123 --> 00:02:31.563
filling of the left ventricle.

38
00:02:32.003 --> 00:02:35.603
This is a mechanism in preterm lambs, with no human outcome.

39
00:02:36.223 --> 00:02:40.643
The study cannot separate a transfusion of blood volume from preload replacement.

40
00:02:41.383 --> 00:02:45.643
Aeration lets pulmonary venous return take over the filling, so the order decides

41
00:02:45.643 --> 00:02:48.143
whether preload falls as afterload rises.
